WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:05.670
Narrator: "Pokémon Detective
Pikachu" marks the first time

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.220
the anime characters have
stepped onto the big screen

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.420
as photorealistic creatures,
but creating these characters

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.450
for the real world without
upsetting diehard fans

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.180
was no easy feat.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.090
It required an army of animators,

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.330
over a year of design collaboration,

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:22.750
a meticulous study of animal biology,

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.513
and even enlisting the
help of a famous mime.

00:00:31.744 --> 00:00:34.750
♪ I wanna be the very best. ♪

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:36.870
Narrator: Animated Pokémon
were first introduced

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:41.870
to the world in 1996, and for
the 2019 live-action movie,

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.340
it took a lot of time and a large team

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.160
of people to get it right
and make sure the fans

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.440
would approve of the updated look.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.700
Pete Dionne: How can we add
additional detailing without

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:55.780
ever breaking the spirit
of the original character?

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:00.100
Narrator: That's Pete Dionne,
VFX supervisor at MPC,

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.150
which worked on "Detective Pikachu,"

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:05.050
as well as other big films
like "The Jungle Book,"

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.480
"The Lion King," and "Godzilla."

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:11.800
MPC Film had over 600 people
working on the project

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.110
with the help of hundreds
more from other VFX companies

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:17.620
like Framestore and Image Engine.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:21.110
The VFX team spent an entire
year designing the characters

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:23.600
before the filming even started,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.900
working closely with The
Pokémon Company in the process,

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.810
which had one main rule.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:30.390
Pete: No matter what situation they're in,

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:31.660
they always have to be cute.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:34.630
Narrator: This meant no
creepy, gnarled teeth,

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:37.730
nasty fingernails, or skin blemishes,

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.310
although they pushed
the limit with Lickitung

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.390
and his slobbery appendage.

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.110
About 60 Pokémon from the original series

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:47.470
were recreated for the film.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:50.470
They started with the
original 2D anime characters

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.530
and video game models as a base.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:55.260
Pete: There's that one
main Pikachu that we see

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.190
in all of the anime that
we really recognize,

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:00.270
and that's the one that
we chose to embrace.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.410
Narrator: Next, they
would find some real-world

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:05.590
animal equivalents or as
close as they could get.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:08.160
For Pikachu, for example,
they looked at animals

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.450
like bush babies and lemurs.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:12.610
Pete: We wanted to represent it as

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:15.400
a very confident quadruped animal.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.270
Narrator: They would then
take the skeletal structure

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:20.840
of these animals and tweak it
to fit the character's shape.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.000
In Pikachu's case, the
skull needed to be enlarged

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.320
and the limbs needed to be elongated.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.310
Once the structure was laid out,

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:30.940
they would then add
layers of fat and muscle.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.440
Pikachu: I'm trying to get
in 100,000 steps this hour.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.690
Narrator: And on top of all that, the fur.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.520
They studied the fur patterns
on animals like rabbits

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.140
to make it look more natural.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:43.320
Pete: When we get really close to Pikachu,

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.530
you really see kind of all that detail.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.280
Narrator: Here's an example
of the various layers

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.900
of creature creation from
another of MPC's past projects,

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.483
"The Jungle Book."

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.310
Next up, movement.

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.970
They started rigging
the characters early on

00:02:57.970 --> 00:02:59.273
in the design process.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.890
Rigging is when you lay out the framework

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.110
for how a character
will move, and for this,

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:06.560
they took their biology
into consideration.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.670
In cartoons, characters
can squash and stretch,

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:11.840
but since these characters
are based in reality,

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.370
they had to rely on real physics
and specific proportions.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:18.527
Pete: We grabbed clips of
bulldog puppies frolicking

00:03:18.527 --> 00:03:20.090
and playing with each other,

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:23.290
animate our Bulbasaur
creature to match that.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:24.270
Narrator: Mewtwo was modeled

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.093
to look like a muscular child,

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.150
but his movements were
heavily influenced by cats.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.920
The most difficult Pokémon to nail down?

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:34.350
Mr. Mime.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:37.170
Pete: With Mr. Mime, we
actually did the opposite

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:38.920
of what we did with all
the other characters,

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:43.400
which was, how do we make this
thing not look like a human?

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.840
Narrator: To accomplish
this, they used recognizable,

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.480
synthetic materials to make up his form.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.700
His shoulders are like
the old red kickballs

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.600
everyone remembers from childhood,

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.340
the body and horns on his head are made

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.180
to resemble worn Nerf football foam,

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.070
and his skin is like silicone,

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:00.913
which absorbs most of the light.

00:04:00.913 --> 00:04:02.029
Pete: I think it worked.
I don't think anyone

00:04:02.029 --> 00:04:03.001
thinks he's a human.

00:04:03.001 --> 00:04:05.250
Man, he's still creepy though.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:06.460
Narrator: And to model his movements,

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:09.410
they actually hired a
real-life mime from New Zealand

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:11.420
named Trygve Wakenshaw.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:13.160
Some of his scenes were rotoscoped

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:15.350
from his actual performances.

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:16.650
When it came to the battle scene

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:19.480
between Mewtwo and Pikachu,
the animators made sure

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:22.690
to stick to canon as much as
possible for their move sets,

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:25.130
each a recreation of the original moves

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.670
but with more realistic
flair influenced by smoke,

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:30.480
plasma, and electricity.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.123
Pete: The end result
was something that felt

00:04:32.123 --> 00:04:36.470
kind of photographically
realistic, but at the same time,

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:39.720
when you kind of compare side
by side with our references,

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.750
they take on a lot of the same qualities.

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:43.930
Narrator: Since Pikachu
is the star of the film,

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:46.630
they spent a lot of work
on his facial features.

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:49.440
They attached a camera
to Ryan Reynolds' head

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.830
and captured 80 different expressions.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:54.090
Next, they looked at the cartoon Pikachu's

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:57.490
range of emotions,
which were very limited.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:59.998
Pete: We took Ryan Reynolds' expressions,

00:04:59.998 --> 00:05:01.600
we took Pikachu's expressions,

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.010
and then we created
kind of our hybrid set.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:05.920
Narrator: So once the team had

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:07.460
the characters nailed down,

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.120
they needed to start integrating them

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.200
into the real world and
the filming process.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.150
Typically with these types of productions,

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:18.070
they first create some
puppets or stuffies,

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:21.610
which are used on set in place
of the animated character.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:23.490
They shoot several takes with the puppets

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:25.670
so the actors have something to work with

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:28.670
and so the animators can get
a sense of important details

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:30.990
like lighting and reflections.

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:33.420
Pete: And often, those
were the best takes,

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.190
and we would use those takes

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:38.040
and just paint the puppeteer out after.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.050
Narrator: Afterwards, they
would shoot clean takes

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.180
without the puppets.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:42.630
For some secondary characters,

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.820
they just made texture
balls with swatches of fur.

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:48.550
To get accurate reflections
in the Pokémon's eyes,

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:51.490
they also took 360-degree
images of the set

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:54.770
and mapped them back into the
orbs, as you can see here.

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:56.870
Pete: And we could see exactly, you know,

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:00.500
in the glass eyes of our Pikachu puppet,

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:02.960
exactly what that environment reflected

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.700
into his eyes should look like.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.870
Narrator: They had to make
sure the real-world canvas

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:10.370
had the right look and feel
to make the cartoonish Pokémon

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:13.740
seem as real as possible
when integrated into it.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:16.270
They shot the movie on 35-millimeter film

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:19.400
instead of digital to add a grittiness,

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.810
and it was filmed on location
in Scotland and England

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:25.190
as much as possible "to ground
the more surreal elements

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:29.150
in a lived-in reality." And
because it's a detective movie,

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:31.430
Ryme City was given a noir feel,

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:34.180
cool and dark colors
with plenty of shadows

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:37.070
and neon lights reflecting
off wet streets.

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.230
Another crucial factor in
blending the characters

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:41.410
with the real world is props.

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:44.950
The team used a mix of both
digital and practical objects.

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:46.340
Pete: We were sticking props everywhere,

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.270
just for Pikachu to bump into.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:49.830
Narrator: For example, in this scene,

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:51.880
when Pikachu's walking around the office,

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.830
there are real strings
tied around the room,

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:57.490
but any strings he touches
are actually digital,

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:58.533
as we see here.

00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:01.970
Anytime you see him pick up a coffee cup,

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:05.090
those are digital, but when
he bumps into something

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:07.580
like a chair, they would
have a puppeteer move it

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:09.240
at the right moment.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.140
For the parade scene at the end,

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:13.300
the team created real balloons

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:16.163
and were prepared to raise
them in the streets of London,

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:20.520
but it was too windy, so they
never got them off the ground.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:23.180
While you still see
these semi-inflated props

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:26.970
in a few shots, the rest are CGI.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:28.880
Another challenging
scene for the animators

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:33.120
that mixed real props and
CGI was the Torterra forest.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:34.580
Most of the first shots were filmed

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:38.200
in a real valley in
Scotland to set the scene,

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.290
but they created massive
rigs for the actors

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:44.070
to move around on, which
were later combined with CG.

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:46.990
The result of all these
techniques and attention to detail

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:49.760
kept the Pokémon true
to their original form

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:52.410
while at the same time
immersing them in a new,

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.610
more real world for fans
of the franchise to enjoy.

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:57.126
Justice Smith: Seeing
the characters realized

00:07:57.126 --> 00:07:58.809
with fur and textures,

00:07:58.809 --> 00:08:01.363
it's so cool. It's like a
childhood dream come true.

